WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.540
From lethal workouts to secret Beck hat-tips,
You Season 3 is packed with tons of sneaky

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.460
easter eggs, call-backs, and pop culture references.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:10.730
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:14.610
I'm Jan and in this video I'm revealing the
top 40 things/ 40 killer details you might

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:16.660
have missed in Season 3 of You.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:19.680
Spoilers ahead, so take care if you're not
caught up.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.880
When Joe becomes obsessed with his neighbour
Natalie, as we see him go off to snoop on

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:27.910
her, he grabs his old trusty stalking cap,
which has been placed on top of an Ernest

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:32.279
Hemingway biography, a nod to how he dressed
up as the famous author for Beck's birthday

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:33.800
party in Season 1.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.180
Why are you wearing a turtleneck?

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:36.180
I'm Hemingway.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:39.750
And it's also a hat-tip to how the author
came up during one of the dates Joe went on

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:43.180
to make Love jealous in Season 2:
You know your Hemingway.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:44.180
Very sexy.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.900
And the seed for Joe's decision to get away
from Love and take Henry with him is planted

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:52.710
in his mind during his conversation with Natalie
who comments that "kids aren't handcuffs".

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:57.030
It's a neat little remark that calls back
to the literal handcuffs that Joe almost killed

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:02.300
Love with in the Season 2 finale, before she
blurted out she was pregnant with his child,

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:03.760
stopping him in his tracks.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.790
He is the only reason I'm alive.

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:11.462
While Joe is creepily stalking Natalie at
the library, he's stood right next to a stand

00:01:11.462 --> 00:01:16.530
of Scott Landon books, a wicked reference
to the Stephen King novel Lisey's Story, which

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:20.110
features a stalker harassing the wife of author
Scott Landon.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:25.221
After Joe murders Marienne's ex-husband Ryan,
as he walks away from the crime scene, he

00:01:25.221 --> 00:01:27.159
sarcastically comments...

00:01:27.159 --> 00:01:28.860
Always stay through Shavasana.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:33.130
...a humorously dark reference to the Yoga
position commonly known as the corpse pose

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:38.360
which involves lying like a dead body on your
back, which is how Ryan ends up right outside

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.070
a yoga studio.

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:43.920
When Joe and Marienne discover they shared
a similar childhood as they were both abandoned

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.250
by their parents, a connection between the
pair begins to form.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:51.230
The beginning of their illicit relationship
is also signalled by the fact they're standing

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:54.210
next a book titled "Truly Madly Guilty".

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:58.770
As Joe tries to keep his marriage with Love
on an even enough keel that she doesn't find

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:03.220
out about Marienne and kill her, he gets his
wife a present of an expensive casserole dish

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:06.440
she's been looking for and it's also filled
with Donut Pal.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:10.710
This is a throw-back to Season 2 when Joe
missed out on a trip for that same fresh strawberry

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:12.520
and cream-filled dessert with Love.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.480
The things that this donut shop does with
fresh strawberries should be considered a

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:16.480
sex crime.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.129
And they were also the same doughnuts Milo
got for Love when she and Joe broke up.

00:02:21.129 --> 00:02:25.519
When Love's mum Dottie drunkenly rolls up
for some Mexican food, the place she goes

00:02:25.519 --> 00:02:30.959
to is called "Tacos de la Madre", which translates
literally from Spanish as "Mother's Tacos".

00:02:30.959 --> 00:02:35.439
The choice of that particular food for her
and Henry certainly isn't a coincidence either,

00:02:35.439 --> 00:02:40.519
because Dottie believes that Love's baby is
Forty reincarnated, and in both the TV show

00:02:40.519 --> 00:02:43.720
and the original books Forty is addicted to
Taco Bell.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:48.099
And, of course, Dottie, like Forty, was on
a bender that moment in the show herself.

00:02:48.099 --> 00:02:51.760
He's the reincarnation of my son, Forty, and
he loves a good taco.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:52.939
Don't you, baby?

00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:57.950
The name of the fictional town where Love
and Joe live, Madre Linda, also reflects Season

00:02:57.950 --> 00:02:59.709
3's emphasis on motherhood.

00:02:59.709 --> 00:03:04.290
In Spanish, it means "lovely or beautiful
or wonderful mother", a reference to how after

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:08.949
moving there, Love initially finds herself
struggling to fit in with seemingly perfect

00:03:08.949 --> 00:03:11.809
mothers like Sherry, a successful momfluencer.

00:03:11.809 --> 00:03:16.230
Joe describes Madre Linda as the safest neighbourhood
in California at the beginning;

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:20.950
but ironically by the end, it's become notorious
for multiple gruesome murders and kidnappings

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:26.040
all supposedly committed by the town's new,
less-than-perfect mother, Love.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:31.049
A new character in Season 3 is Theo, who has
a touch of Joe about him with his feminist

00:03:31.049 --> 00:03:36.650
pretences and somewhat obsessive romantic
pining and pushy gestures towards Love.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:41.700
Similar to Joe, Theo imitates iconic romantic
movie moments such as the scene from the 80s

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:46.359
movie "Say Anything" where John Cusack holds
up a boombox to prove his love.

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:51.389
According to all the rom-coms of your generation,
this is what the guys do to get the girl.

00:03:51.389 --> 00:03:55.609
What Theo didn't realise though is that Love
isn't that kind of girl.

00:03:55.609 --> 00:03:57.409
I hate rom-coms.

00:03:57.409 --> 00:03:58.469
Take away my ovaries.

00:03:58.469 --> 00:04:02.979
In the penultimate episode, after Theo figures
out Joe was driving Natalie's car the day

00:04:02.979 --> 00:04:07.059
she disappeared, he goes on to discover that
the Conrads are currently locked up in the

00:04:07.059 --> 00:04:09.090
basement of Love's bakery.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:13.709
It's at this point we get to see the "Expand
Your Mind" T-shirt he's been wearing, a nod

00:04:13.709 --> 00:04:18.760
perhaps to what a mind-blowing experience
all these discoveries have been for Theo.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:23.440
When Sherry and Cary eventually find the key
that Love hid in the cage, it turns out it

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:27.840
was hidden inside a tub of vanilla powder,
an ironic little detail given what Sherry

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.770
called Love and Joe just before she started
looking.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:32.620
Those two-faced, vanilla pieces of..

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:37.470
Notice also that the brand name on the tub
is French, Le Meilleur, which means 'The Best',

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:40.120
fitting given Love's perfectionism when it
comes to cooking.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:42.080
This is the best bakery in town?

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:43.080
Shoddy lamination.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:44.230
Definitely didn't use French butter.

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:48.510
Both Joe and Beck mocked the affectionate
term "babe" in the first season.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:51.760
You said "babe" was a default for unimaginative
lovers.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:55.880
And Love also hated the term when Milo used
it with her in the second season

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.330
If you call me "babe" again, I'm gonna stick
you in the oven.

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:02.270
So, it's more than coincidence that when Sherry
and Cary arrive for the swinging session with

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:05.090
Joe and Love, they start using the term with
each other..

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:07.081
Babe, I'm all over it.

00:05:07.081 --> 00:05:11.010
..making it a little signal on top of the
many others of how the Conrads are really

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:12.710
worlds apart from the Quinn-Goldbergs.

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:15.790
God, these people are so much more f!£$%^&amp;
up than I thought.

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:19.980
You's writers have a bit of fun with the dramatic
trope of Chekhov's Gun when Joe discovers

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.540
Cary brought a gun to the swinging session.

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:24.350
This is a Chekhov situation now, isn't it?

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:26.630
If I touch it, I all but guarantee it gets
fired.

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:32.500
The trope is fulfilled when Love gives it
to the Conrads who end up using it twice,

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:38.410
[and] leaving Joe regretting not having it
himself when he's getting beaten up by Ryan.

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:40.880
New trope: wishing you had Chekhov's gun to
fire.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.890
The use of the trope in Season 3 also calls
back to the second season when Forty drunkenly

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:49.080
explained his feature film idea at Henderson's
party.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:50.170
It's Chekhov's knife.

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:54.720
And as soon as we see it, you know throats
are gonna get cut.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:06:00.020
Forty was right because when we saw Love expertly
using a knife to butcher a piece of meat early

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:04.750
in the second season, it was a big clue to
the twist that she'd be revealed as a knife-slashing

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:07.440
serial killer, similar to Joe.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:11.620
Joe and Love's distrust of each other comes
to a head in their last meal together when

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:15.920
Love cooks him a roast chicken, a call-back
to when they first met and she took Joe on

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:20.890
a culinary tour of LA in search of his favourite
food which ended up being the roast chicken

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:22.830
she cooked for him that night.

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:23.830
Perfect bite?

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:24.830
Perfect.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:28.840
The idea of Joe's "perfect bite" also came
up in Season 3 when he was feeling guilty

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:32.650
about Love making him delicious food while
he was thinking about Marienne.

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:33.650
Perfect bite.

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:34.650
Ready?

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:37.750
I don't think it's a coincidence that in the
library scene where Joe thinks Marienne is

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:42.480
starting to flirt with him, she's also putting
away a book with the title "Vessel", a signal

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:46.770
that she's about to become yet another vessel
for Joe to project his fantasies on to,

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:51.860
his latest "You", the next in his parade of
replaceable, interchangeable women from Candace

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:56.531
to Beck, Love, and Natalie, who he can obsess
over and fill with his idealized view of what

00:06:56.531 --> 00:07:01.030
a perfect woman should be, then toss aside
when they don't meet his manufactured version

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:02.139
of them.

00:07:02.139 --> 00:07:06.960
Joe's warped opinion of himself as a good
man, a hero even, comes up when he compares

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:11.150
himself to the fictional character Atticus
Finch from the Pulitzer-prize-winning book

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:12.150
To Kill A Mockingbird.

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:14.800
You're a steady, stalwart Atticus Finch.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:19.340
This idea is reinforced later on when Love's
mother Dottie says Joe looks "like a young

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:23.550
Gregory Peck", the actor who played Finch
in the 1962 adaptation of the novel.

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:29.680
Marienne, however, makes a much less flattering
comparison when she makes fun of Joe's look

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:32.830
as he arrives at work after a photo-shoot
with Sherry.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:39.180
Whole vibe's very Patrick-Bateman-meets-Venetian-gondolier.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:43.650
Marienne is on the money, of course, in comparing
Joe to the fictional serial killer and unreliable

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:48.400
narrator from American Psycho and later on
in the finale she reveals she did have her

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:50.300
suspicions about him.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:54.650
When Love is thinking about having a second
baby with Joe - a daughter she hopes - she

00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:59.490
seems to settle on the name "Julia" which
is interesting as Marienne's daughter is called

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:01.980
"Juliette" which means "little Julia".

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:06.650
Of course, Joe's name begins with a "J" too,
and in a flashback we also discovered that

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:11.240
Joe's mother had a new son called Jacob after
she gave Joe up.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:16.330
Perhaps like Joe's mother then, Love is subconsciously
trying to replace Juliette, the daughter of

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:19.270
Joe's new obsession, with her own little girl.

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:24.040
A shout-out to Alaïa who noticed that when
Joe takes on the pseudonym Nick at the very

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:27.980
end, it's likely a reference to Nick Carraway
from The Great Gatsby.

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:32.250
Similar to Joe, Nick is the narrator and a
character in F. Scott Fitzgerald's famous

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:36.289
novel, and there have been many other Great
Gatsby references through the series.

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:40.539
For instance, Joe reads from the novel to
Henry in the opening episode.

00:08:40.539 --> 00:08:45.529
In Season 1, Joe and Beck refer to themselves
as Fitzgerald and his wife Zelda, and then

00:08:45.529 --> 00:08:49.680
there's Forty's habit of calling Joe "old
sport" like Gatsby does.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:54.620
Joe taking on the name Nick might additionally
be a subconscious reference to Dr Nicky who

00:08:54.620 --> 00:08:58.870
he framed for Beck's murder and who I think
could be coming back for Season 4 which I

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:02.850
talk a lot more about in my Season 4 theories
video.

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:06.700
As Matthew waits for Theo, he reads about
the events in what the media's dubbed the

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:08.730
Quinn-Goldberg "butcher house".

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:14.050
An article in real-life online women's magazine
The Cut reveals that Love's reported murder-suicide

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:19.240
has inspired memes, art and even a K-Pop viral
hit titled "Pie4Me/Die4Me",

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:24.300
a nod to part of Joe's body being baked in
a pie, akin to the infamous human meat pies

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:28.399
made by Mrs Lovett in the tale of the serial-killing
barber Sweeney Todd.

00:09:28.399 --> 00:09:33.550
Apparently Love's original name for her bakery
was "LoveMuffin", both a euphemism and a throw-back

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:37.410
to how she used to leave those sweet treats
in Joe's locker at Anavrin.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:38.410
Muffins?

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:40.589
Yeah, I, uh...
seem to remember owing you one.

00:09:40.589 --> 00:09:44.420
The article explains that Love wasn't able
to clear the rights to the name, which could

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:50.019
be a real-world in-joke as it is actually
the name of a singles website in real life.

00:09:50.019 --> 00:09:54.440
And curiously, it's also the name of the antagonist
group in Disney's animated Phineas and Ferb.

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:55.839
Just use the safe word:
Hakuna matata.

00:09:55.839 --> 00:09:57.079
Disney should sue these people.

00:09:57.079 --> 00:10:01.910
The article's accompanying photo is from Love
and Joe's wedding and its depiction of a blood-covered

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:06.550
cake with a bride murderously bashing the
groom from behind is a humorous call-back

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:09.189
to Kiki's cupcake order at the top of the
episode:

00:10:09.189 --> 00:10:12.670
Kiki: Hey, can you make two dozen bloody-looking
ones by tonight?

00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:15.300
I'm having my true-crime book club over to
dish.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:19.970
Both Love and Joe's use of the poison wolfsbane
on each other is a twisted reference to the

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:23.439
special love language they created between
themselves in Season 2.

00:10:23.439 --> 00:10:24.439
I wolf you.

00:10:24.439 --> 00:10:25.620
I wolf you too.

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:30.320
And thanks to 'Valar Morghulis' for pointing
out that Wolf is also even the brand of their

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:34.009
oven, which Joe uses to burn Love and their
home to the ground.

00:10:34.009 --> 00:10:38.180
And for a full breakdown of more details you
might have missed in the Season 3 finale,

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:43.480
tap here to watch my Ending Explained video
or follow the link in the video description.

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:47.269
Just before the credits roll in the first
episode, a screen appears in honour of actor

00:10:47.269 --> 00:10:52.350
Mark Blum, who played Mr Mooney, Joe's surrogate
father and bookstore owner in New York.

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:56.939
Blum sadly died in March 2020 from complications
arising from Covid-19.

00:10:56.939 --> 00:11:00.740
So, what other details did you notice in Season
3?

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:02.399
And what were your favourite moments?

00:11:02.399 --> 00:11:03.949
Let me know in the comments below.

00:11:03.949 --> 00:11:08.519
If you enjoyed this video, then a like and
a share are hugely appreciated.

00:11:08.519 --> 00:11:13.480
You can tap left for my full You playlist
including the Season 3 ending explained or

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:18.130
tap right for my Season 4 theories including
whether Love could still be alive.

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:20.389
Thanks for watching and see ya next time.

00:11:20.389 --> 00:11:21.999
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

